{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1315637744/16/codegen_prompts/3.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1315637744/16`\n- 题号：`3`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1315637744/16/3.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"3\">\n- `description`：在网格中找到由两种颜色交替铺设的棋盘格区域，识别其中被\"污染\"偏移了一格的子块，将该子块回移到正确的棋盘格位置。\n- `Input structure`：背景色网格中有一个大的矩形区域由两种非背景色交替填充形成完美棋盘格图案。该棋盘格区域中有一个矩形子块（边界与棋盘格对齐）被整体平移了恰好一格（上、下、左或右），导致该子块内的颜色与周围棋盘格不再对齐——子块边缘出现连续同色的相邻像素对（正常棋盘格中不会出现）。子块平移后露出的原位置被背景色填充。\n- `Rule`：\n  1. 识别网格中的棋盘格区域和其中被偏移的矩形子块（通过检测棋盘格中出现同色相邻对的区域来定位子块边界）。\n  2. 确定子块的偏移方向（子块移动后在原位留下了一行或一列的背景色间隙，间隙在子块的反方向侧）。\n  3. 将子块反向平移一格，回到原来位置，恢复完美棋盘格。\n  4. 输出裁剪为棋盘格区域的外接矩形。\n- `Key insight`：解题者不能直接\"看到\"子块的边界——必须通过检测棋盘格图案中哪些地方出现了奇偶性违例（同色相邻）来反推子块的范围和位移方向。这是一个\"通过图案破缺定位异常\"的推理过程。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：train/e9fc42f2, eval/dbff022c, train/d93c6891, train/8dab14c2, train/6cbe9eb8, train/d968ffd4, eval/88e364bc, train/fe45cba4\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e9fc42f2\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3085\nmechanism=0.3319\nlexical=0.0139\ncode_feature=0.6667\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, code:output_resize, object:grid_edge, operation:crop_resize, operation:translate, output:crop, relation:adjacent, relation:direction\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/e9fc42f2.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/e9fc42f2.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nAssemble the foreground objects by docking equal-colored boundary markers edge-to-edge.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background color and several 4-connected multicolor foreground\nobjects.  One foreground color is the body color; the less frequent foreground colors are\nboundary connector markers, and each marker color appears once as a free end or twice as a\nmatched docking pair.\n\nRule\nCrop each foreground object to its bounding box and translate the objects so that the two\noccurrences of every paired connector color touch on their exposed boundary sides.  Preserve\neach object's orientation and colors, then return the tight crop around the assembled\nforeground on the original background color.\n\nKey insight\nThe colored marker pixels are ports: matching colors specify which objects must touch, and\nthe marker's side of its object determines the direction of the translation.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"4-connected foreground object sprite s with boundary connector markers\",\n  \"dst\": \"matching boundary connector marker d on another sprite\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.marker.color == d.marker.color and the two marker pixels have opposite exposed sides\",\n  \"transform\": \"translate sprites so each paired marker touches edge-to-edge; preserve sprite colors and orientation\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"tight crop of the assembled foreground on the original background color\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"dbff022c\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2929\nmechanism=0.2857\nlexical=0.0133\ncode_feature=0.8000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, code:output_resize, object:closed_region, object:grid_edge, operation:flood_fill, relation:adjacent, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/dbff022c.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/dbff022c.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nFill hollow sprites' interiors using a color-palette legend on the grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Several hollow sprites with distinct border colors.\n- A small 2×N or N×2 palette block sits on one grid edge; one row/column lists border colors, the other lists corresponding fill colors.\n\nRule:\n- Locate the palette legend on a grid edge and read it as a border→fill color mapping.\n- Find enclosed bg regions (holes) inside sprites that don't touch the grid boundary.\n- For each hole, identify the most common adjacent border color, look it up in the palette, and fill the hole with the mapped fill color.\n\nKey insight: The palette's edge position and orientation determine reading direction; border colors in the palette uniquely key into fill colors.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Use a color palette legend on the grid edge to fill sprites' interiors based on their border color mapping.\",\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"hollow sprites with colored borders s\",\n  \"dst\": \"interior of same sprite d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"d.fill_color == palette_lookup(s.border_color)\",\n  \"path\": \"\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"\",\n  \"transform\": \"fill d with palette[s.border_color]\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": \"palette is 2xN or Nx2 block on grid edge; read direction based on edge position: top edge reads top-to-bottom, bottom edge reads bottom-to-top, left edge reads left-to-right; first row/column is border color, second is fill color\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"Step 1: locate 2xN or Nx2 palette legend on grid edge; Step 2: build color mapping (border -> fill) based on reading direction; Step 3: for each hollow sprite, lookup border color in palette and fill interior with corresponding fill color\",\n  \"concepts\": [\n    \"color-mapping\",\n    \"lookup-table\",\n    \"flood-fill\",\n    \"border-detection\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"The challenge is correctly reading the palette legend based on its edge position and orientation\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d93c6891\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2981\nmechanism=0.2931\nlexical=0.0140\ncode_feature=0.8000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, code:output_resize, object:grid_edge, object:rectangle, operation:translate, relation:adjacent, relation:aligned, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/d93c6891.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/d93c6891.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nMove each gray bar into its adjacent orange rectangle as full stripes from the contacted edge.\n\nInput structure\n- The scene uses black outside space, yellow fill, gray one-cell-thick bars, and solid orange rectangles.\n- Each gray bar 4-touches exactly one orange rectangle; some orange rectangles have no touching gray bar.\n- Gray bars touching the same orange rectangle all have the same orientation.\n\nRule\n- Recolor every original gray cell to yellow.\n- For each orange rectangle touched by gray bars, count the gray cells assigned to that rectangle.\n- Horizontal gray bars repaint that many cells as complete orange rows starting from the contacted top or bottom row.\n- Vertical gray bars repaint that many cells as complete orange columns starting from the contacted left or right column.\n- Orange rectangles with no touching gray bar remain unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The gray pixels are not copied by shape; their count is transferred into the neighboring orange rectangle as edge-aligned row or column stripes.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"gray one-cell-thick bars s\",\n  \"dst\": \"4-adjacent solid orange rectangles d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s 4-touches d\",\n  \"transform\": \"erase s to yellow; in each matched d, repaint |matched s cells| orange cells gray as complete rows for horizontal bars or complete columns for vertical bars from the touched edge\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8dab14c2\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2670\nmechanism=0.3003\nlexical=0.0139\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:output_resize, object:grid_edge, operation:translate, relation:adjacent, relation:aligned, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/8dab14c2.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/8dab14c2.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nComplete each boundary tab or notch by adding the complementary feature on the opposite side of the foreground object.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has exactly two colors: a dominant background and one foreground object.\n- The foreground object is a thick rectilinear shape with one-pixel tabs and notches on its boundary.\n\nRule\n- A foreground tab is a one-neighbor boundary pixel; project through its full row or column span and remove the opposite edge pixel to make a matching notch.\n- A background notch is a one-pixel cut with three foreground 4-neighbors; project across the full row or column span and add a tab just outside the opposite edge.\n- Notches immediately beside a tab source are not independent sources, because that gap belongs to the tab's local boundary shape.\n\nKey insight\n- The changed pixels are not color-specific noise; they are paired jigsaw-like boundary features on opposite sides of the same thick row or column cross-section.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"one thick monochrome foreground object on a dominant background, with one-pixel boundary tabs and missing edge pixels\",\n  \"transform\": \"for each foreground tab, remove the opposite edge pixel on the same row or column; for each missing edge pixel, add a foreground tab just outside the opposite edge, ignoring gaps immediately beside a tab source\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6cbe9eb8\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2666\nmechanism=0.2821\nlexical=0.0141\ncode_feature=0.6000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:output_resize, object:rectangle, operation:crop_resize, operation:translate, output:crop, relation:direction\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/6cbe9eb8.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/6cbe9eb8.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nRebuild the foreground rectangles as a largest-to-smallest lower-left nesting.\n\nInput structure\n- A periodic wallpaper background is overlaid by foreground colors that each form a rectangular frame or solid rectangle.\n- The largest foreground rectangle is the output frame; smaller foreground rectangles are the objects to nest inside it.\n- A frame may be partially hidden by a smaller rectangle, but its visible pixels still determine its bbox.\n\nRule\n- Ignore the wallpaper and crop the result to the largest foreground rectangle's bbox size.\n- Sort foreground rectangles by bbox area from largest to smallest.\n- Reconstruct each frame or solid rectangle, placing every next smaller rectangle in the lower-left of the current rectangle's usable region.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
